<%# The one brand mark, fixed top-left, on every surface of every app.
    Type only — no chip, no border, no background. It is a link home and it is
    the one piece of chrome that is identical everywhere, which is why it lives
    here rather than three times.

    Before this, the three apps did three different things: brgen had a fixed
    type wordmark, amber rendered a non-fixed SVG (whose own comment describes
    "retro hairlines"), and bsdports had no mark at all — only a text link inside
    its nav. The SVG is still the right thing for a landing or marketing surface;
    it is the wrong thing for chrome that has to sit in a 44px corner on every
    page.

    <header> wraps it so the wordmark is inside a banner landmark — axe flagged
    it as the one piece of page content outside any landmark. The header adds no
    box: the mark is position:fixed, so its parent has no in-flow child and
    computes to zero height. Do not hang layout off it.

    Params:
      label — the wordmark text. Defaults to the app name.
      href  — where home is. Defaults to the app's root_path. %>
<% mark_label = local_assigns.fetch(:label) { t("app.name", default: "brgen") } %>
<% mark_href  = local_assigns.fetch(:href) { main_app.respond_to?(:root_path) ? main_app.root_path : "/" } %>
<%# brgen passes its historical class alongside so every rule already written
    against .brgen-logo-mark keeps applying. %>
<% mark_class = local_assigns.fetch(:class_name, "brand-mark") %>
<header>
  <%= link_to mark_href, class: mark_class, aria: { label: t("nav.brand_home", default: mark_label) } do %>
    <span class="brand-text"><%= mark_label %></span>
  <% end %>
</header>
